# What the client says after it has stopped on its own.
#
# A fault used to be invisible from the device: the process died, the launcher came back, and
# the only trace was a log on the card that stopped mid-sentence. The user's whole report was
# "it crashed", and there was nothing to ask them for.
#
# So a crash now leaves a file, and the file is announced. This scene is the announcement and
# the press that clears it - the two halves that make it a banner rather than a warning nobody
# can act on.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 220

tab nodes
hold 900

# The previous run faulted. The banner is the error family rather than the loader's warning:
# nothing is broken about a radio waiting in its bootloader, and something was plainly broken
# about a client that stopped by itself. It costs rows, because it has settled - the list below
# gets what is left, exactly as it does under the update banner.
crash on
hold 1800

# It follows across the tabs, because it is not about any of them. What it is about is the
# client, which is the whole test for whether something belongs up here.
tab status
hold 1200
tab messages
hold 1200

# And it stands down inside the one section that says the same thing in more detail - the same
# rule that keeps the update banner out of About, and the app bar's overline from repeating the
# tab name underneath it.
tab settings
key a
hold 1600

# The two rows it was pointing at. The value column is two dozen cells, so the row names the
# file rather than its path - the Data row above points at the directory, and a filename is the
# half that survives being drawn at any scale. The verb below is what makes the notice
# resolvable, which is the condition every banner here has to meet before it may be raised.
key down 3
hold 1400

# Pressed. The report is deleted, the rows go with it, and the banner has nowhere left to be.
key a
hold 1800

# Back out, and the frame is quiet again - which is the proof that it resolved rather than
# merely being scrolled past.
key b
hold 1200
tab nodes
hold 1400
